e  which is in a vitrine  with deep  sensual eyelids she's absolutely  heavenly and she made up for  she made up for it for me uh look her up  bartelboine uh maori choice i've  forgotten her name um  but in any event uh that's the first  part of it i think museums just have to  be careful about things such as that  it's an interesting thing in the history  of art and that's a tangent there are  some major figures who actually are  better image makers and they are  painters  i would say that for example uh  of um  um  uh  the the belgian surrealist  magnet magritte is a bad painter but  he's a fabulous image maker  and so on and so forth but  the mayor is a great painter don't make  him a good image maker okay  the other part of it is a much more  complicated  issue that of the government the cuts to  the arts and this and so forth  um  i exercise my meter in a country with  all museums except the smithsonian in  washington are private there are no fun  the met doesn't receive any money from  washington it receives a little bit from  the city of new york for guardianship  but if we said tomorrow to this day we  wouldn't need it you wouldn't notice it  so it's it's it's minimal uh it's  entirely based on the money given by  a great many donors many of whom are on  the board of trustees this is why we put  them on the board of trustees  and  well it's just a fact of life  and  the issue here is that i cannot  altogether condemn  and  having  been born french and also a country  where all of the museums are our state  institution i've certainly worked  closely i'm on the board of trustees of  the museum say i've just come from a  meeting there  where the government is also  cutting subsidies to a number of places  these governments and the museums are in  a conundrum because on the one hand  there is ingrained in the society in the  minds of all of you  that well if you do not disperse any  money somehow the state will  that is not an incentive for you to go  into your 